            contentEn: 'Here the same honesty is required as in the previous chapter. The details of Freud\'s theory largely did not survive modern scientific criticism. The psychosexual stages, penis envy and the Oedipus complex cannot be tested empirically, and many psychologists rejected them.</p><p>But the big idea did hold up, and became a cornerstone: <strong>early experiences in infancy and childhood shape the adult over the long term</strong>. Everything that will come in this chapter, Bowlby\'s attachment, Winnicott\'s environment, Erikson\'s stages, is built on the assumption Freud laid down first. <strong>He may not have been precise in the details, but he opened the question</strong>.</p><p>And there is a trap here worth identifying in advance. If everything is sealed in childhood, it is easy to conclude that change is impossible. Freud himself did not think so, since the entire therapy he developed was intended precisely to free people from these patterns. And this is the question that will accompany the whole chapter: <strong>how much of us has already been written, and how much is still open</strong>.',
