            contentEn: 'Bowlby is strong exactly where Freud was weak: <strong>his idea was tested empirically</strong>. Mary Ainsworth, who worked with him, built in the 1970s an experiment called <strong>"the Strange Situation"</strong>, in which one observes how a toddler reacts when the parent leaves the room and returns. From the reactions consistent attachment patterns emerged, <strong>secure, anxious and avoidant</strong>, which were observed again and again in hundreds of studies around the world. This is no longer a hypothesis about hidden mechanisms in the mind, but a pattern that can be measured.</p><p>But two caveats are needed, because this idea is usually presented in a version that is too rigid. First, <strong>the early model is not a verdict</strong>. Longitudinal studies show that it changes, and a reliable later relationship with a therapist or a partner can rewrite it. Second, attachment is not tied to the mother alone, as Bowlby initially thought, but to any stable figure who responds consistently.</p><p>And here the question for the next dialogue opens. Bowlby showed that a reliable bond is needed, but what actually makes an environment good enough? Winnicott will argue that the answer is surprising: not perfect parenting, but parenting that fails to the right degree.',
