            contentEn: 'The idea sounds paradoxical, but it has an internal logic. The repetition is not a pursuit of pain, it is <strong>an attempt to finish something that remained open</strong>. Each time anew, with an unconscious hope that this time it will end differently.</p><p><strong>A real-life example:</strong> a person who grew up with a cold and unattainable parent, and finds himself drawn again and again to precisely those distant partners. <strong>He is not choosing pain and does not enjoy it.</strong> He returns to the only arena in which the undecided battle can still be won: this time I will manage to make someone like that love me.</p><p>And here lies the explanation for a familiar phenomenon: <strong>precisely when something feels "familiar" and "right" immediately, it is worth pausing.</strong> That feeling is not necessarily a sign of a good fit. Sometimes it is a sign that we have recognized an old pattern.',
