            contentEn: 'Freud took this idea to a place that mostly did not survive. He concluded from repetition compulsion that there exists in us a <strong>death drive</strong>, a fundamental tendency to return to a state of motionlessness. Many of his students rejected this idea, and many do not accept it today.</p><p>But <strong>the phenomenon itself survived well</strong>, even if the explanation did not. It is known by other names: traumatic re-enactment among sufferers of post-trauma, and in everyday language "patterns" and "cycles". The difference is that today it is commonly explained differently: not as a death drive, but as early learning that was deeply engraved, as a system that identifies the familiar as safe, and as templates the brain activates automatically without checking whether they still fit.</p><p>And here the chapter opens. Freud described the phenomenon and gave it a name. The rest of the thinkers will try to explain how it actually works. Melanie Klein will argue in the next dialogue that we do not repeat only events, but carry the figures themselves inside us.',
